 * via {@code SecureRandom#setSeed(byte[])}.  Supplying a predictable or attacker‑controlled
 * seed weakens the randomness and can lead to cryptographic compromise.  This implementation
 * removes that capability entirely and relies on the JVM's built‑in, cryptographically
 * strong seeding mechanism.</p>
 *
 * <p>If a custom algorithm is configured it will be used; otherwise the default
 * {@link SecureRandom} constructor (or {@code getInstanceStrong()} on newer JDKs) is used.
 * The instance is "warmed up" with a single call to {@code nextBytes} to ensure any lazy
 * seeding performed by the provider is triggered before the bean is handed to callers.</p>
